sk group is composed of  technical experts from the industry who  are  working to develop a standard for common  use  and then the draft document  is circulated within the task group  expert panel who then  agrees on a final draft  which is used to perform a pilot study  based on  astm d 6300 criteria  the results of the pilot study are then  used to design  the inter-laboratory study which  also has requirements  and are also stated in astm d  6300 the interlaboratory study  is for the purpose of collecting  test data which is then processed  for statistical analysis and the results  are published as the precision and bias  statement of the standard test method  once the task group completes their work  and their uh as agreement on occasion  there's some  unofficial voting within the task group  to assure that consensus at the task  group level  is met however it's unofficial and in  most cases  any dissensions or disagreements on  technical matter are addressed  at the task group once there is  agreement  however at the task group level the  proposed standard test method is  submitted to the subcommittee  for balloting balloting is performed  electro  by electronic basis  and it normally takes approximately 30  days  the ballot must have a 60 return rate  with two thirds approval in order to  move forward  now i'll not mention  the items that may hold up or  cause the ballot to fail and those are  negative votes i'll explain that  in the detailed review of the standard  promulgation process  if the  ballot passes that subcommittee  then the item the proposed test method  is then balloted at main committee  which has the same criteria and it  also has the same 30-day balloting  process  if it's approved that main then it moves  to  committee on standards for final review  and it also has a 30-day ballot review  with due process and it also has an  appeals process  at that point  so that is the general hierarchy of  how a standard moves through the astm  process  th