 year  the inter laboratory study which  is a much more rigorous  study required  that each laboratory test three fuels  two for crude oil and residual oil and  the biodiesel samples that  interlaboratory study was completed in  march of  2019  both of these proposed standards  were submitted for subcommittee ballot  in may  of 2019 they were concurrently performed  all three of these intra-laboratory  studies there was an interlaboratory  study conducted for crude oil  one for residual fuel oil and one for  biodiesel  they were completed and then in  started and added to the proposed test  methods as the precision for each  proposed test method  that ballot submitted in may of 2019  received a negative vote  which required adjudication and  resolution  the negative vote could not be resolved  without significant changes  to to the standard and in this case  the majority of the concerns or negative  comments  were related to the description  of the working range  and the valid test result range defined  as new items  in astm d6300  the astm d 6300 defines the precision  and bias  criteria as well as the working range  and the valid test result range that  caused  confusion and significant changes  were required to the  proposed test method so we had to reball  it  in july of 2019  that ballot also generated  additional negatives  and i adjudicated that negative  in at the stated meeting in december of  2019  that negative was found non-persuasive  therefore the proposed standards  moved on as as far as d8322 is concerned  it moved on to main committee ballot now  at this time the  second standard had to be re-balloted at  subcommittee for a third time  so that's not shown here but it started  falling behind  then the main committee ballot  produced no negative votes there was one  item  that was resolved and the standard  d8322 was published in july  of this year so that's an example  of a collaboration  of two standards performed by the same  collaborating team usi