rmative  or negative if there is a negative vote  it requires a comment  as a technical concern regarding  the proposed standard  if there are no negatives  the ballot with the proposed ballot item  with the proposed  standard moves on the main committee  ballot however  if there are any negatives the rules of  the balloting process  state that all negatives must be  resolved  before it can move on to the main  committee ballot  so if there is a negative  the technical contact  for the work item works to resolve any  negatives  and comments and then  there's a decision tree  if the negative comment is persuasive  but it is a change that is not editorial  then the standard is revised and sent  back to the  subcommittee for rebalancing  if through the resolution process  that proposed or required change  concerning the negative is an editorial  or if the negative is withdrawn after  discussion  or in the worst case  if the negative voter refuses to work  with the technical contact in resolving  that  the negative vote  it is possible to find  the negative vote non-persuasive  that process requires presentation of  uh adjudication at a stated meeting  during the june meeting or the december  meeting  so if the vote is editorial  and can move on or if the negative vote  is withdrawn or if it's found  non-persuasive then it can move on  to main committee for balloting  the main committee balloting process  requires  30 days and again it goes through the  same process  during the balloting process if you have  negative votes and comments they have to  be addressed  and the same decision tree is used  to resolve negatives as in the one that  was described for the subcommittee  if there are no negative votes it  me it moved the standard test method  moves on to the committee on standards  for review and then  published after that  so the big question as i mentioned most  people have  regarding astm standards and and some  other  standard development organizations it  takes time to develop a standard  and the big question is why does it take  so long  and the answer is it takes as long as it  takes  and as i mentioned also  the each standard test method is unique  it also as you may consider  requires resources it requires requires  materials it requires instrument time  it requires a shipment of samples to  each location and  then more importantly it requires a  dedicated  effort of a collaborator to run  the samples for both the pilot study  and the inter laboratory  study so all of that  work as you might imagine requires a  significant amount of time  and in the process there are  an unknown amount of variables that  require time  to complete so in this process  as you notice the timeline the pre-work  performed by the task group  in the process of method development and  establishing the precision  through a precision study and all of the  work i just mentioned  requires x amount of time  and i i can't  begin to express that the more complex  methods require  a significantly more amount of time  and then the work item begins at time  zero that's  as the work item is presented and then  of course then you have the balloting  process that subcommittee ballot  at about six months and then the main  committee ballot if it passes directly  to maine that's 12 months  and then review and publication  so the  fastest possible completion is one year  you have the technical presentation the  work item  and if the standard has already been  worked  to a point where the precision study and  all of the work  has been completed to to that point then  it can be completed in one year  but more typical it requires between 18  to 36 months  to establish and publish an astm  standard  now there's also additional  time required because at  the five year mark after publication or  last revision  there is a review of the standard  that means that the standard must be  reviewed by  the subcommittee and re-balloted for  a re-approval so all standards at astm  are reviewed at least every  at minimum every five years in order to  be  continued in publication  so you see these are all variables that  answer the question why does it take  so long to publish a standard  as you can see there's a significant  amount of work for each standard  required  what i'd like to do now is provide a  collaboration example  of one that i am personally acquainted  with  we at agilent started a collaboration  to establish two astm standards as you  may see there is  astm standard for crude oil the  elemental analysis  of crude oil and residual fuel oil  by mpaes  that standard was published this past  july a couple of months ago  and  at the same time a standard  test method was in promulgation  concurrently at the same time  as the one that has already been  published so that gives you an  idea of the complexity of even  using the same platform the mpaes  to run two different three different  types of  fuels the collaborating team is shown  there  it was a global collaboration with 12  laboratories performing the pilot study  and the inter laboratory precision study  that is our collaborating team shown at  the  bottom left now what i'd like to do is  briefly go over the timeline  for the development of this standard  the program timeline to establish the  8322  we began the and presented the technical  presentation  at the astm meeting in june of 2017.  in that meeting we requested two work  items  one for what is now d8322  and one for the second proposed standard  work item 56916  that proposed standard is still in the  balancing process  the pilot study for  this test method was completed in july  of 2018.  the work to establish and get to  a pilot study as you can see is just a  little over one year  and that's coordinating shipment of  pilot study  well preparing the samples for the pilot  study  and everything involved with  distribution and testing  completion and processing the  statistical data  it required approximately one year  the inter laboratory study which  is a much more rigorous  study required  that each laboratory test three fuels  two for crude oil and residual oil and  the biodiesel samples that  interlaboratory study was completed in  march of  2019  both of these proposed standards  were submitted for subcommittee ballot  in may  of 2019 they were concurrently performed  all three of these intra-laboratory  studies there was an interlaboratory  study conducted for crude oil  one for residual fuel oil and one for  biodiesel  they were completed and then in  started and added to the proposed test  methods as the precision for each  proposed test method  that ballot submitted in may of 2019  received a negative vote  which required adjudication and  resolution  the negative vote could not be resolved  without significant changes  to to the standard and in this case  the majority of the concerns or negative  comments  were related to the description  of the working range  and the valid test result range defined  as new items  in astm d6300  the astm d 6300 defines the precision  and bias  criteria as well as the working range  and the valid test result range that  caused  confusion and significant changes  were required to the  proposed test method so we had to reball  it  in july of 2019  that ballot also generated  additional negatives  and i adjudicated that negative  in at the stated meeting in december of  2019  that negative was found non-persuasive  therefore the proposed standards  moved on as as far as d8322 is concerned  it moved on to main committee ballot now  at this time the  second standard had to be re-balloted at  subcommittee for a third time  so that's not shown here but it started  falling behind  then the main committee ballot  produced no negative votes there was one  item  that was resolved and the standard  d8322 was published in july  of this year so that's an example  of a collaboration  of two standards performed by the same  collaborating team using the same  instrument platform however  one of the work items moved on to be  published as d8322  and the second work item is still  invalid  and as of this moment today i'm working  on  some negative votes at the main  committee so that gives you an idea of  the challenge  that we're up against when we're  establishing an astm standard  and it can also provide some insight  to the robustness of the process at astm  so the current standards in development  at astm  those that i am working on  are shown here now the first one is  there  basically because it just went from  work item status to published astm  standard  and that is the standard test method  for determination of elements in  residual fuels and crude oils by  microwave plasma atomic emission  spectroscopy  the mpaes that was published in july  and we've moved on  the next item  is the second standard which is still in  work item status  the work item is 56916  again the only difference there is that  the test method is for determination  of elements in biodiesel blends up to  b100  if we work through to resolve the  negatives  currently on ballot i hope to complete  that by the end of this year in december  the other item that i am working on  is at committee d2  dot 4 subcommittee m  for mass spec the  test method for work item  50453 is the standard test method for  determination of chemical species  in marine fuel oil by multi-dimensional  gas chromatography mass spectrometry  we are currently in the process of  conducting an inter-laboratory study  for the precision and bias statement of  that standard  d7845 in addition we have a second work  item with a request that we received  for expansion of the scope  so that's work item 56072  the expansion of the scope for this test  method  includes components which cannot be  performed  by the current sample preparation  procedure  so that requires twofold  requires a new sample preparation  procedure  as well as an interlaboratory study  when we complete that the goal  is to develop the new procedure  for the additional components and  incorporate  the new procedure as procedure b  with the current procedure in d7845  as procedure a i am currently  collaborating with intertek  on that work  then we also have uh some joint work  with bureau veritas as  we revise astm  d8110 at present  d8110 was published with conditional  provisional precision data  and it's for the elemental analysis of  metal distillates by icp mass spec  the goal is to expand the scope of the  element table capabilities  and to include crude oil analysis by icp  mass spec  so those are the standard test methods  that i am working  on and moving at  astm  so one note and to make sure  the information that i provided is based  on my experience  and the resources that i have at  astm.org  i would recommend and suggest  that you join astm  possibly attend the biennial meetings if  you're interested in participating  and also and more important as you  might have noticed there's a significant  amount of work  performed by the task groups and as  as a technical contact for multiple  standards  it is uh challenging to find  participating labs  and i and i truly understand why because  i've managed laboratories  in the past at contract labs and  i know the volume of work that is  performed  in most laboratories  it makes it difficult to add additional  load of sample testing  to participate in an ils but  it's vitally important to continue to  expand the volunteer list  so that we can have additional support  and resources that help to develop  standards  so you can go to the website astm.org  and get all of the details required  beyond  what i obviously have provided here  and in closing the disclaimer is that  this is not inclusive of what  is required to establish a standard it  is merely  an overview and if you need  additional details you may contact  the staff at astm.org  thank you for your time today i believe  we're moving on to the questions and  comments  section of the presentation have a good  day  thank you george for that outstanding  presentation  we will now move to the live q a portion  of the presentation  as a reminder please submit your  questions via the q  a box we welcome all questions now  george our first question from our  audience member  is are all the subcommittees organized  the same way yes  uh for the most part the the difference  is going to be  with the task groups uh some are are  more specialized  in in terms of being able to address  the requirements for each specific  standard as i mentioned  all of these standards have different  procedures different apparatus  and different capabilities so  the task group can expand and  and you know may have on the order of  100 or or so  collaborators participating in the task  group  so effectively they're all the same it's  just the  size and the number of participants in  each task group  and what about membership do we have to  be a member of  astm to participate  so that if  the person is is interested  in reviewing and  the standards as they're valid in the  electronic process  you do need to be a member for that  process and  when you join uh you select the  committee which would be  this case d2 and also you need to select  the subcommittees that you're interested  in and they're all listed  on the website so once you do that  as ballots are um  rolled out electronically you will  receive an update  and as you can access the ballot on on  the astm website  so that part you do have to be a member  however  there are quite a few labs you know as i  mentioned  for the intro interlaboratory studies  not all of the folks that are  participating running the samples  and even for the expert panel  they're not all members of astm they're  folks that volunteer their time  to develop a standard that can be used  in their lab and  and shared for the industry  so for participating within the task  group  and all of that work uh donating  chemicals and reagents and  you know other things all of that is  effectively the majority as i mentioned  the majority of the  work to develop a standard you can also  participate in drafting the method and  adding your comments and your experience  and your expertise you can join the task  group  but you do not have to be an astm  member for that  george gonzalez thank you again for your  presentation today  and for your important research and i  want to thank our audience  audience for our outstanding questions  throughout the conference  any questions that were submitted and  not answered today by our speaker  will be addressed via email and this  presentation will be available for  on-demand viewing  through september 2021 please share it  with a colleague who may  be interested in today's topic and thank  you again for your participation  we hope you enjoyed the agilent science  and technology virtual symposium  until next time stay safe stay healthy  bye everyone bye
 

